That can be a genuinely useful cognitive strategy if by selectively compartmentalize you mean deliberately holding different problems, roles, assumptions, or emotional material in separate mental contexts and choosing when to reconnect them.

In the language we’ve been building, the interesting part is the selectivity:

\text{Compartmentalization}
=
\text{controlled boundary formation}

\text{Integration}
=
\text{controlled boundary crossing}

The powerful capability isn’t maximum separation. It’s reversible separation: isolate something enough to reason about it without interference, while retaining a reliable route back to the larger context.

That gives a nice systems principle:

\boxed{\text{Healthy compartment} =
\text{boundary}+\text{address}+\text{return path}}

Without the return path, isolation can become fragmentation. With it, the compartments become more like sandboxes: separate execution environments that can later exchange validated outputs.

And that connects directly to the Semantic Reacharound/Wrestling Mat idea: sometimes you deliberately separate perspectives precisely so you can later reassociate them from a different direction and discover relationships that weren’t visible while everything was blended together.